WEBVTT

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.680
Our world is not always the same.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.650
Hidden from our view lies a different world.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:17.860
Creatures utterly unlike us, almost alien.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.110
Yet they are more numerous than any other group on the planet.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:29.140
Welcome.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:40.460
the fascinating world of the arthropods spiders scorpions and insects today we have new camera

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:47.910
techniques that will allow us to reveal in greater detail than ever before their lives the way they

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:56.380
fight and feed and reproduce this series uses specially developed 3d camera technology to study

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.880
the micro world in extraordinary detail both

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.790
location and in specially constructed environments. We'll witness their births, the challenges they

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:13.310
face and the moments when their lives hang in the balance. And that may help us understand

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:22.860
how it is that today over 80% of all animal species on this planet are arthropods. In this

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:29.960
series we'll see the way they have evolved from the comparative simplicity of the millipede to

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.720
Vast colonies that contain hundreds, even millions of individuals.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.240
We'll witness the most extraordinary transformations in the animal kingdom.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.380
We'll meet ants that farm, spiders that can cast their webs,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.040
and the bug that wears the bodies of its victims as a disguise.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:58.520
Welcome to a strange and dangerous world.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:23.060
the arthropod adaptations the most revolutionary has been the ability to live in immense colonies

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:29.980
that enables them to hunt en masse to build huge constructions for a home and to

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:46.180
dominate their surroundings. Some colonies are quite small, others contain as many individuals

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:54.650
as our largest cities. If great numbers of individuals are to work together, they need

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:58.480
to be able to communicate, to pass on information and instruction.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:05.120
And doing that enables them to maintain farms,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:09.290
to plunder the forest floor like an invading army,

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.440
and to build immense castles.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:29.970
Maybe workers are able to send...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.700
complex messages to one another.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:38.610
In the wild, they sometimes nest out in the open,

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:43.770
but mankind has persuaded them to live and store their honey in hives.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.240
The colony's heart is its queen.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:53.050
She is just a little bigger than her subjects and mother of them all.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.950
In spring, when food stocks are low, the workers get busy collecting nectar.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:26.670
They have a remarkable method of telling one another where to find the most productive flowers.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.580
It's called the waggle dance.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:42.880
This returning bee has just found a new source of nectar

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.640
and is going to tell others in the hive about it.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.160
First, she gathers an audience.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.980
To do that, she climbs on her sister's backs and...

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:08.570
vibrates her abdomen. Now that she's got their attention, she begins her dance using a code of

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:23.760
movements that tell her fellow workers where her discovery lies. The duration of her waggle

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:29.980
indicates the distance to the nectar source. The longer the waggle, the further the flow.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.900
and the angle at which she dances across the cone

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:38.960
tells them the direction to the flower in relation to the sun.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:46.900
Her instructions are remarkably accurate

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:51.830
and can pinpoint the location of a nectar source over six kilometres away.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:58.000
Some of her fellow workers set off immediately to find it.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.530
In one short season, the colony's workers will visit up to 500 million flowers

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:09.710
and will make around 90 kilograms of honey.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:14.520
That is sufficient to sustain the whole colony through the coming winter

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.740
when there is no nectar to be had.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.500
But dancing can only communicate with a small number of individuals.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:29.980
In the forests of Africa...

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:33.200
There are communities a thousand times larger than that.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:38.150
For much of the time, they're dispersed,

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:42.350
ranging through the forest in dozens of columns, searching for prey.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:54.060
Driver and colony may contain 50 million individuals,

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:56.280
and they're virtually all blind.

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:03.650
Their community has no permanent home,

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:07.770
just a series of temporary bivouacs.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.240
Ord is coordinated by the queen.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:24.980
Unlike her honeybee equivalent,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.980
she is many times larger than her workers.

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:44.310
Her size enables her to produce at least 120,000 eggs a day.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:55.110
She is tended by the workers when in a bivouac

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:58.200
and carried by them when the time comes to move on.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.760
Soldiers with huge jaws guard the traveling workers and attack prey when they find it.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.880
A colony of 50 million needs a lot of food.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:19.850
The ants communicate by releasing and smelling chemicals called pheromones.

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.680
Earlier in the day, a scout found a good hunting site.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.320
and marked out a path to it by laying a trail of pheromones on the ground.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:43.490
Hunters followed the trail, sensing it with their antennae.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:48.420
Soldiers guard the flanks of the rushing column,

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:53.480
while the smaller workers, who will butcher and transport their victims, run down the middle.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.140
Those at the head of the column will tackle anything that is too slow to escape.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:13.610
They have found a slug and released a different phenomenon,

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.090
this time into the air, signalling that they need help.

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:26.610
Workers and soldiers from all over the area rush in for the kill.

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.970
The soldiers' powerful jaws slice into the slug.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:48.710
Fragments of it are sent back to the Queen and workers waiting in the bivouac.

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:58.870
Within minutes, nothing is left of the slug.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:04.820
By communicating with pheromones, a colony scouring the forest

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:08.510
can collect hundreds of thousands of victims in a day.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.720
That is enough to keep the queen and her millions of subjects well fed,

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:20.990
so she can continue on her own particular task

00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:25.110
of producing enough offspring to maintain the size of the community.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:40.940
An organized community of millions can only work if individuals within it can communicate with one another.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.960
Out on the sun-baked floor of the Rift Valley in East Africa,

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:08.730
daytime temperatures can rise to 40 degrees or more,

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.330
and there's little or no shade.

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:14.000
So the townites that live there make it for themselves.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:21.300
They build air-conditioned castles.

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:38.290
Queen lives in a special chamber about a meter below the surface of the ground.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:47.070
By her side, a single fertile male, her king, the father of the colony.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:57.680
The fleshy abdomen is distended with eggs.

00:11:59.590 --> 00:11:59.970
Her tiny...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:14.440
head dwarfed by her huge body. Soldiers guard the royal chamber, their pincers raised ready to tackle

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:24.060
intruders. She's so huge she can't move by herself and has to be tended by specialist workers who

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:25.580
continually groom her.

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:35.580
She produces eggs almost continuously.

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:39.530
Attendant workers take them away as soon as they arrive.

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:47.510
She can lay thousands a day, 165 million over her 15-year life.

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:56.510
But to produce this prodigious number, she needs perfect conditions,

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:59.780
a steady temperature and a constant supply.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.560
of well-oxygenated air.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:06.340
If she doesn't get that, she will die.

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:08.340
And with her, the colony.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:12.060
Since she herself can't move,

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:15.100
the workers have to create the conditions that suit her.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:23.310
They've done so by building an air-conditioning system,

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:27.830
a maze of chimneys and towers that stand above her chamber.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:37.920
It can be nine meters tall, but despite the mound's huge size,

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:41.310
not a single termite lives in it permanently.

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:43.930
They stay underground.

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:48.370
The sides of the castle are studded with holes.

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:56.570
Animations show how gusts of wind move across the savannah.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:13.590
hot air blows into these empty holes the ventilation passages within have many twists and turns

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:19.020
that slow down the air and as it slows beyond the reach of the sun's rays

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:28.580
it cools fresh air dispersing through the mound displaces the old stale air

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:36.230
Outside, it's over 40 degrees centigrade,

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:40.850
but in the Queen's underground chamber, a comfortable 27.

00:14:43.090 --> 00:14:47.850
Working together, these tiny insects have created a cool, air-conditioned home,

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:58.480
something they could never have done, working as separate individuals.

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:13.900
In Central and South America, in the rainforests, other immense insect communities have achieved something perhaps even more remarkable.

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:24.020
These are leafcutter ants, and their underground nests are gigantic.

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:29.200
They can be 30 meters across and contain 8 million individuals.

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:37.430
And they owe their success to something they devised long, long before we did, agriculture.

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:48.790
Leaf cutters have found a way of harvesting the vast proliferation of leaves produced by the forest trees.

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:56.800
They remove them piece by piece, but they don't eat them.

00:15:57.200 --> 00:15:59.460
In fact, they can't even digest them.

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:09.920
The leaves are fodder for their underground farms.

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:23.260
Like all complex colonies, the leaf cutters have a central organising individual.

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:27.840
Their queen is many times larger than the workers.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:29.980
When she founded the colony...

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.200
she brought with her a tiny piece of fungus that now grows in gardens throughout the nest.

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:44.760
As it grows, the fungus produces little white knob-like structures which are full of nutrients

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:47.400
which the ants can digest.

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:59.900
Out in the forest, foragers cut the leaves into segments and carry them back to the nest.

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:17.680
they have sharp powerful jaws which slice through the toughest leaves pieces they cut

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:26.310
can be as much as 50 times their own body weight they can be so heavy

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:29.970
that sometimes only the larger major cast of the ants can

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:44.630
lift them. Their nest may be up to 120 meters away, a very long distance for a

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:50.520
porter that is only a centimeter long. Here the smallest cast of ants in the

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:57.350
community, the Minims, are hard at work in the underground gardens. They receive

00:17:57.350 --> 00:17:59.980
the leaves from the foragers, chew them up and

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.470
feed them to the fungus. They're very fastidious and constantly check to make

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:11.040
sure that the gardens are kept clean and properly watered. They also control the

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:16.060
quality of the leaves sent to them by the workers. If it suits them they release a

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:26.360
pheromone which encourages the workers to cut more. If they dislike what they're

00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:29.830
getting they release a different pheromone that stops

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:49.360
the collection of that kind of leaf. A single colony of some eight million individuals could

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:55.510
harvest a fifth of the new leaves grown each year by the trees in the surrounding forest.

00:18:57.370 --> 00:18:59.530
Millions of closely related individuals.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.620
have become a single, integrated super-organism.

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:09.580
The arthropods are the most successful animals on the planet.

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:13.190
We already know of over a million different species,

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:15.650
and we're discovering more every day.

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:17.900
And the reason is quite simple.

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:21.280
They've had over 400 million years

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:26.580
in which to evolve new ways of feeding and fighting and collaborating.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:29.960
And the result is the dazzling range of species.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:32.040
that we see on Earth today.

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:34.790
Quite simply, the arthropods

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:36.850
are the most successful kind of animal

00:19:36.850 --> 00:19:38.030
on this planet.

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:42.140
In this series, new technology

00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:44.600
has enabled us to look at arthropods

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:45.780
in a different way

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:48.850
and reveal how they have adapted

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:51.300
over hundreds of millions of years

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:55.300
from the simple, solitary lifestyle

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:56.400
of the millipede

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:59.850
to the vast colonies containing

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:08.380
millions of individuals we have traced the fascinating techniques they've evolved in order

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:18.200
to survive from the gruesome disguise of the assassin bug to the bombardier beetles superheated

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:27.000
defenses we've seen how a wasp can subdue a cockroach and turn it into a helpless living

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:28.740
food source for her young

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:38.120
Watch the extraordinary mating dance of the scorpion and the praying mantis

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:41.510
that sacrifices his life in order to reproduce.

00:20:45.560 --> 00:20:50.920
All are the product of the same simple drives that underpin all life.

00:20:57.880 --> 00:20:59.220
The need to eat.

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:04.770
to survive and to reproduce.

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:10.850
Some care for their young.

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:18.660
Others transform themselves in order to find a mate.

00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:26.310
And some create gigantic communities

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:29.790
that numerically rival our greatest cities.

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:36.270
And thanks to today's extraordinary technology, we're beginning to understand them better.

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:42.740
We like to think that we share the planet with the arthropods,

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:48.380
but you could argue that this planet is more theirs than ours.
